1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,564
Somewhere in Bangalore, a Swiggy engineer is staring at a dashboard.

2
00:00:04,249 --> 00:00:13,108
Numbers are scrolling past — twelve thousand something, forty-five milliseconds, eight-twenty milliseconds, ninety-nine point nine nine percent.

3
00:00:13,793 --> 00:00:15,848
To you and me, that's noise.

4
00:00:16,126 --> 00:00:20,236
To this engineer, it's the entire health of the system, at a glance.

5
00:00:20,666 --> 00:00:27,492
So what do these numbers actually mean, and how does one glance tell you if everything's fine, or about to fall over?

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,631
Here's the trap: a single number, with no vocabulary around it, doesn't tell you anything.

7
00:00:35,130 --> 00:00:37,127
"820" — is that good?

8
00:00:37,626 --> 00:00:38,102
Bad?

9
00:00:38,253 --> 00:00:39,217
Milliseconds?

10
00:00:39,368 --> 00:00:40,157
Requests?

11
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:41,121
You can't tell.

12
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:44,662
The vocabulary is what turns a number into a diagnosis.

13
00:00:45,091 --> 00:00:46,694
That's what this episode gives you.

14
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,049
You already met two of these numbers last episode, so this is just a reminder, not a re-teach.

15
00:00:55,850 --> 00:00:59,461
qps: how many requests the system handles every second.

16
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:06,903
p50 and p99: how long a request takes, for the typical user and for the unlucky one-in-a-hundred.

17
00:01:07,495 --> 00:01:12,870
Today we go one level deeper on both, and we add a third number that neither of them tells you.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:24,242
First correction: "a lakh people opened the app" and "the server is handling a lakh requests a second" are not the same sentence.

19
00:01:25,333 --> 00:01:28,444
A hundred thousand people can have Swiggy open right now.

20
00:01:29,036 --> 00:01:34,354
But most of them are just staring at the menu, deciding between biryani and butter chicken.

21
00:01:34,853 --> 00:01:39,299
They're not hitting the server every second, they're idle, most of the time.

22
00:01:39,984 --> 00:01:46,080
Only the ones actually tapping, searching, ordering, refreshing, generate real requests.

23
00:01:46,765 --> 00:01:52,070
That gap between "people online" and "requests per second" is called think time.

24
00:01:52,871 --> 00:01:58,107
So a hundred thousand concurrent users might only produce two thousand qps.

25
00:01:58,699 --> 00:02:06,315
Confusing the two is how people wildly overestimate, or underestimate, what a server actually needs to handle.

26
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,065
Second new idea: it's not enough to know your qps.

27
00:02:11,495 --> 00:02:13,909
What matters is how close you are to the edge.

28
00:02:14,409 --> 00:02:22,188
If your system can handle ten thousand qps and you're sitting at two thousand, you have headroom, plenty of room before anything breaks.

29
00:02:22,559 --> 00:02:25,566
At seventy-five hundred, you're getting close.

30
00:02:26,251 --> 00:02:29,862
Response times start creeping up even before you hit the ceiling.

31
00:02:30,547 --> 00:02:41,158
Cross the ceiling, and it's not a graceful slowdown, queues start forming, and things that used to take milliseconds start taking seconds.

32
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,775
Now the third number, the one from the cliffhanger.

33
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:50,303
Engineers promise reliability using something called "nines.

34
00:02:50,303 --> 00:02:52,497
" Ninety-nine percent uptime.

35
00:02:52,996 --> 00:02:54,227
Ninety-nine point nine.

36
00:02:54,656 --> 00:02:56,142
Ninety-nine point nine nine.

37
00:02:56,572 --> 00:02:58,395
Ninety-nine point nine nine nine.

38
00:02:58,894 --> 00:03:01,170
Every extra nine sounds like a rounding error.

39
00:03:01,669 --> 00:03:04,177
It is absolutely not one, and here's why.

40
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,642
Let's translate percentages into something you can actually feel: minutes and hours a year.

41
00:03:12,235 --> 00:03:18,992
Ninety-nine percent uptime sounds great, until you realize it allows three point six five days of downtime a year.

42
00:03:19,793 --> 00:03:24,819
That's a whole long weekend of "the app is down," spread across the calendar.

43
00:03:25,319 --> 00:03:32,029
Add one nine, ninety-nine point nine percent, and that shrinks to eight point seven six hours a year.

44
00:03:32,621 --> 00:03:35,919
Better, but still an entire workday of outages.

45
00:03:36,348 --> 00:03:41,073
Add another nine and you're down to fifty-two minutes a year, total.

46
00:03:41,875 --> 00:03:44,359
One more nine, and you're at five minutes a year.

47
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:49,015
Five minutes, across the entire year, for every outage combined.

48
00:03:49,816 --> 00:03:53,693
Each nine you add is roughly a ten-times reduction in allowed downtime.

49
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:03,790
Here's why this isn't just a trivia stat, because some systems literally cannot tolerate the outage a "lower" nine allows.

50
00:04:04,882 --> 00:04:13,195
Every UPI payment in India, from paying an auto driver to a business settling lakhs, runs through this rail.

51
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:20,451
NPCI, the body that runs UPI, mandates ninety-nine point nine nine percent uptime for banks on the network.

52
00:04:21,136 --> 00:04:29,461
Not ninety-nine percent, that extra two nines is the difference between "rare glitch" and "money stuck mid-transfer during peak hours, at scale.

53
00:04:29,461 --> 00:04:36,334
" Three point six five days of a payment rail being unreliable would be a national story.

54
00:04:37,263 --> 00:04:39,445
Fifty-two minutes a year is survivable.

55
00:04:39,945 --> 00:04:46,063
That gap is exactly why the nines matter here, they're a design requirement, not a stat you round off.

56
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,601
So why doesn't everyone just build for five nines?

57
00:04:51,402 --> 00:04:54,142
Because each extra nine gets brutally more expensive.

58
00:04:54,734 --> 00:05:00,725
Going from ninety-nine to ninety-nine point nine might mean better monitoring and a faster on-call process.

59
00:05:01,526 --> 00:05:10,407
Going from ninety-nine point nine nine to five nines can mean an entire second data centre, standing by, just for the days everything else fails at once.

60
00:05:10,999 --> 00:05:13,379
The reliability curve is steep at the top.

61
00:05:13,971 --> 00:05:18,557
Chasing the last nine is often the most expensive engineering work a company ever does.

62
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:27,594
One more piece of vocabulary, because you'll hear these three constantly: SLA, SLO, and SLI.

63
00:05:28,279 --> 00:05:29,975
SLA, service level agreement.

64
00:05:30,567 --> 00:05:35,153
The promise made externally, often with a penalty attached if it's broken.

65
00:05:35,954 --> 00:05:37,765
SLO, service level objective.

66
00:05:38,450 --> 00:05:46,809
The internal bar the engineering team actually aims for, usually stricter than the SLA, so there's a safety margin.

67
00:05:47,610 --> 00:05:49,990
SLI, service level indicator.

68
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:55,296
The real number being measured right now, that tells you whether you're meeting the SLO.

69
00:05:55,888 --> 00:05:57,386
The SLI is reality.

70
00:05:58,071 --> 00:05:59,894
The SLO is the internal goal.

71
00:06:00,393 --> 00:06:02,564
The SLA is what you promised the world.

72
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,702
Let's go back to that dashboard from the start of the episode, except now you can actually read it.

73
00:06:10,132 --> 00:06:15,751
Twelve thousand qps, that's the load, and you now know to check it against the ceiling for headroom.

74
00:06:16,552 --> 00:06:23,785
Forty-five milliseconds typical, eight-twenty for the unlucky one percent, that's the felt experience.

75
00:06:24,284 --> 00:06:29,126
Ninety-nine point nine nine percent, that's the promise being kept, or broken.

76
00:06:29,718 --> 00:06:30,577
Three numbers.

77
00:06:31,169 --> 00:06:32,191
Five seconds.

78
00:06:32,562 --> 00:06:34,629
The entire health of the system.

79
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,149
Let's put it to work.

80
00:06:38,521 --> 00:06:43,560
Same 8pm Swiggy rush from last episode, but now watch the numbers, not just the chaos.

81
00:06:44,152 --> 00:06:47,089
qps climbs toward the ceiling, headroom is disappearing.

82
00:06:47,890 --> 00:06:54,716
p99 latency, which was already the "unlucky" number, spikes hard, now even more people are having a bad time.

83
00:06:55,309 --> 00:07:04,109
And because this outage is dragging on, the month's uptime number is dropping in real time, eating into the whole year's downtime budget in one evening.

84
00:07:04,794 --> 00:07:07,244
An engineer watching this doesn't need to guess.

85
00:07:07,836 --> 00:07:12,805
The vocabulary tells them exactly what's happening and how bad it is, instantly.

86
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:21,095
Here's the trade-off this episode lands on: more nines isn't free, and it isn't always worth it.

87
00:07:21,687 --> 00:07:27,156
An internal analytics dashboard breaking for twenty minutes a month is annoying, not a crisis.

88
00:07:27,527 --> 00:07:30,790
A payment system breaking for the same twenty minutes is a headline.

89
00:07:31,289 --> 00:07:35,434
The right number of nines depends entirely on what breaks if you don't have them.

90
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,194
Three numbers, one vocabulary.

91
00:07:39,786 --> 00:07:43,188
qps: how much load the system is under, right now.

92
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:49,620
p50 and p99: how the request feels, for the typical user, and for the unlucky one.

93
00:07:50,119 --> 00:07:55,158
And nines: the promise being kept, measured in minutes of downtime a year.

94
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,389
But here's the thing, knowing these numbers doesn't fix anything by itself.

95
00:08:01,981 --> 00:08:03,850
They just tell you that something's wrong.

96
00:08:04,651 --> 00:08:07,704
So what do you actually do when the numbers say you're out of room?

97
00:08:08,981 --> 00:08:16,923
Next episode: the load balancer, the waiter who decides which kitchen gets your order, so that no single cook ever gets buried.
